Optimal Algorithm Selection Under Uncertainty:
A Rigorous Framework with Calibrated LLM Priors and Formal Guarantees

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract
We present a rigorous theoretical framework for online algorithm selection that injects a large language model (LLM) prior into a contextual bandit while providing distribution‑free coverage guarantees. The selector is a stochastic policy constrained by an adaptive conformal prediction set that, with high probability, contains the instance‑wise optimal algorithm. At each round, Conformalized Thompson Sampling (CTS) draws an arm from the posterior restricted to the conformal set. We prove three formal guarantees: (i) long‑run conformal coverage of the optimal arm at a user‑specified rate, valid without exchangeability; (ii) a Bayesian regret bound whose leading constant is the entropy of the optimal arm under the LLM prior—hence the prior strictly improves upon a uniform prior exactly when H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) < \log K; (iii) a worst‑case frequentist regret bound of \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{KT}) that holds for any environment, ensuring safety even when the LLM is profoundly wrong. We formalise the architecture with a linear contextual posterior, clarifying all statistical assumptions, and provide complete proofs. The contribution is a formally bounded synthesis of an LLM‑derived prior, adaptive conformal inference, and Thompson sampling, offering a theoretical foundation for trustworthy AI‑assisted algorithm selection. No empirical experiments are conducted; we close with a research agenda.

---

1. Introduction

The algorithm selection problem (Rice, 1976) asks: given a problem instance P from a space \mathcal{P}, which algorithm A from a portfolio \mathcal{A} = \{1,\dots,K\} should be applied to minimise cost? A classical selector is a deterministic mapping \mathcal{S}: \mathcal{P} \to \mathcal{A}—it cannot express uncertainty, hedge against misselection, or provide formal guarantees. In many real‑world settings (SAT solver selection, sorting algorithm choice, machine‑learning hyper‑parameter tuning) the features of P are only partially observed, algorithm performance is stochastic, and the cost of a wrong choice may be asymmetric. Deterministic selectors lack calibrated confidence and offer no principled way to incorporate prior knowledge.

Large language models (LLMs) trained on vast corpora of algorithmic literature, benchmark results, and code repositories carry implicit knowledge about which algorithms suit which problem families. However, this knowledge is uncalibrated—LLMs can be overconfident on instances far from their training distribution, and their confidence scores do not reflect true empirical correctness probabilities (Guo et al., 2017). A recent study (arXiv:2602.21947) demonstrates that frontier LLMs exhibit “algorithmic blindness”: they systematically fail to translate declarative knowledge into calibrated expectations about empirical performance. This observation challenges the very premise of using an LLM as a prior.

We propose a framework that addresses both difficulties simultaneously. The selector is a stochastic policy that tightly couples a calibrated LLM prior with a Bayesian bandit that learns from experience. The framework does not assume the LLM prior is useful; instead, it provides formal conditions under which it is, and proves that the architecture remains safe even when the prior is harmful. The system delivers three concrete guarantees:

· Calibration: An adaptive conformal prediction set \mathcal{C}_t contains the true optimal algorithm with a long‑run average miscoverage rate bounded by \alpha + \mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{T}).
· Regret optimality: If the Bayesian model is well‑specified, the cumulative regret is bounded by \mathcal{O}\big(\sqrt{K H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*)\, T}\big). The prior strictly improves upon a uniform prior precisely when its entropy over the optimal arm, H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*), is smaller than \log K.
· Worst‑case robustness: For any cost environment, the regret is \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{KT}), independent of the LLM prior’s quality.

Scope and theoretical nature. This paper is a purely theoretical contribution. We fully specify a linear contextual Bayesian model, state all assumptions, and provide complete proofs. No empirical experiments are conducted; the paper establishes a mathematical foundation and concludes with a research agenda for future empirical validation.

The novel contributions are: (i) the integration of adaptive conformal inference with Thompson sampling for algorithm selection, (ii) the information‑theoretic Bayesian regret analysis that yields an exact condition for the LLM prior to be beneficial, and (iii) a frequentist safety guarantee that makes the framework deployable even in the face of “algorithmically blind” LLMs.

