2. Problem Setting and Formal Model

We model algorithm selection as a contextual stochastic bandit with a finite set of arms (algorithms) \mathcal{A} = \{1,\dots,K\}.

Instance stream. At each round t = 1,2,\dots, nature draws a problem instance P_t from an unknown and possibly non‑stationary distribution \mathcal{D}_t over \mathcal{P}. The selector observes a feature vector x_t = \phi(P_t) \in \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d produced by a fixed, known feature map \phi. The features are assumed to encapsulate all information relevant to algorithm performance:

Assumption 1 (Feature Sufficiency). The conditional mean cost of any arm a given the instance P depends only on x = \phi(P). Formally, \mathbb{E}[C_t \mid P_t, A_t = a] = \mu_a(x_t) for some function \mu_a: \mathcal{X} \to [0,1].

Arms are chosen by the selector; costs are bounded in [0,1]. The optimal arm for context x is a^*(x) = \arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mu_a(x) (ties broken arbitrarily). The expected regret over T rounds is

R_T = \sum_{t=1}^T \big( \mu_{A_t}(x_t) - \mu_{a^*(x_t)}(x_t) \big).

Linear contextual model. We adopt a linear structure on the means. For each arm a, there exists an unknown parameter vector \theta_a \in \mathbb{R}^d such that for all x \in \mathcal{X},

\mu_a(x) = x^\top \theta_a.

We assume \|\theta_a\| \le S for all a and \|x_t\| \le L almost surely. To ensure \mu_a(x) \in [0,1], we may restrict \mathcal{X} and the parameter space accordingly; the linear model is a standard assumption in contextual bandit theory (Li et al., 2010; Agrawal & Goyal, 2013). The analysis is easily extendable to logistic models.

LLM prior. The LLM, when prompted with a description of the features x, outputs a probability vector \pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}(\cdot|x) \in \Delta_K. We interpret this as a prior belief over the identity of the optimal arm. To use it in a Bayesian bandit, we must define a prior over the parameters \theta_a. A simple and flexible choice is a Gaussian prior for each arm’s parameter, with mean informed by the LLM. Let \Sigma_{0,a} be a fixed positive‑definite covariance matrix (e.g., \lambda^{-1} I_d). We set

\theta_a \sim \mathcal{N}(m_{0,a}, \Sigma_{0,a}),

where m_{0,a} is chosen such that the implied prior probability that arm a is optimal for a given context x is large when \pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}(a|x) is large. Concretely, one can calibrate the prior means by requiring that, for an average context x from the training distribution, the softmax of the prior mean scores \{x^\top m_{0,a}\}_a matches \pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}(\cdot|x) in expectation. This is a straightforward offline procedure; the exact mapping is secondary to the analysis. The prior entropy of interest, H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*), is the entropy of the random variable A^*(x) when x is drawn from \mathcal{D} and the parameters \theta are drawn from the prior. It can be estimated via Monte Carlo before deployment.

Bayesian model. The full probabilistic model assumes that for each round t, given x_t and the chosen arm A_t, the cost C_t is drawn from a Gaussian \mathcal{N}(x_t^\top \theta_{A_t}, \sigma^2) truncated to [0,1] (or one may use a logistic likelihood; the regret proofs are virtually identical under bounded noise). The noise variance \sigma^2 is known. The set of all observations up to time t is denoted \mathcal{H}_t. The learner maintains the posterior distribution p(\theta \mid \mathcal{H}_t); because the prior is Gaussian and the likelihood is Gaussian (with clipping), the posterior is approximately Gaussian and can be updated efficiently.

Conformal calibration. To provide coverage guarantees for the optimal arm, we assume the availability of a small initial set of labeled instances for which the true optimal arm is known. This is a delayed oracle assumption:

Assumption 2 (Initial Calibration Set). Before round 1, a set \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{cal}} of n i.i.d. pairs (x_j, a^*(x_j)) drawn from the initial distribution \mathcal{D}_0 is available. During the online phase, after a sufficient number of rounds, the true optimal arm for a subset of past instances is revealed (e.g., by exhaustive offline benchmarking). We treat this revelation as occurring with a fixed delay d; for the coverage analysis we assume d=0 to keep notation simple, but the results extend gracefully.

With this assumption, we can maintain a sliding window of the most recent fully labelled instances and construct conformal prediction sets for the optimal arm using the LLM’s score. Our procedure uses Adaptive Conformal Inference (ACI; Gibbs & Candès, 2021) to automatically adjust the quantile threshold to achieve a target miscoverage rate \alpha in the long run, even under distribution shift.

