4. Formal Guarantees

We now present the three main theorems. Full proofs are deferred to the appendices. We first list the technical assumptions used in the analysis.

Assumption 3 (Bounded features and parameters). \|x_t\| \le L, \|\theta_a\| \le S for all a, t.

Assumption 4 (Sub‑Gaussian noise). The costs satisfy C_t = x_t^\top \theta_{A_t} + \eta_t, where \eta_t is conditionally \sigma-sub‑Gaussian.

Assumption 5 (Bounded information ratio). For the prior and the Gaussian linear model, Thompson sampling satisfies \Gamma_t \le K/2 for all contexts, where \Gamma_t = \frac{\mathbb{E}_{t-1}[R_t]^2}{I_t(A^*; (A_t, R_t))}.

This bounded information ratio is a known property of Gaussian linear TS (Russo & Van Roy, 2014, Section 5.2).

---

4.1 Long‑Run Conformal Coverage

Theorem 1 (Coverage Guarantee).
Under Assumption 2 (initial calibration set and delayed labels), for any instance sequence and any choice of \gamma > 0, the ACI procedure in Algorithm 1 ensures

\left| \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^T \mathrm{err}_t - \alpha \right| \le \frac{1}{\gamma T} + \gamma.

Choosing \gamma = 1/\sqrt{T} yields \frac{1}{T}\sum_t \mathrm{err}_t \le \alpha + \frac{2}{\sqrt{T}}. Thus the long‑run miscoverage rate of the conformal set converges to \alpha at rate \mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{T}), without any exchangeability assumptions on the instance stream.

Proof sketch. The ACI update is equivalent to online subgradient descent on the absolute loss |\bar{\mathrm{err}}_t - \alpha|. Standard online convex optimisation bounds give the result (Gibbs & Candès, 2021, Theorem 1). ∎

---

4.2 Bayesian Regret and the Prior Utility Condition

We now assume the Bayesian model is well‑specified: the instance stream is i.i.d. from a fixed distribution \mathcal{D}, and the parameters \theta_a are drawn from the LLM‑informed Gaussian prior. The Bayesian regret is \mathbb{E}[R_T], where the expectation is over the prior, the instance draws, and the algorithm’s randomness.

Theorem 2 (Bayesian Regret of CTS).
Under Assumptions 1–5, the Bayesian regret of CTS (without forced exploration, \varepsilon = 0) satisfies

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \sqrt{2 K \, H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) \, T} + T \Big( \alpha + \frac{2}{\sqrt{T}} \Big).

If the target miscoverage is set to \alpha = c/\sqrt{T}, the regret is \mathcal{O}(\sqrt{K H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) T}).

Proof sketch. Decompose the regret into rounds where the optimal arm is inside the conformal set and those where it is outside. On the former, the truncation does not exclude a^*, so CTS performs standard Thompson sampling. The information‑theoretic analysis of Russo & Van Roy (2014) yields \mathbb{E}[R_T^{\mathrm{in}}] \le \sqrt{\frac12 K \mathbb{E}[I(A^*; \mathcal{H}_T)] T}. The mutual information is bounded by the prior entropy H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*). Out‑of‑set rounds contribute at most the number of such rounds, which by Theorem 1 is bounded by \alpha T + 2\sqrt{T}. ∎

Corollary 1 (Prior Utility Condition).
Let R_T^{\mathrm{LLM}} denote the Bayesian regret under the LLM prior, and R_T^{\mathrm{unif}} the regret under a uniform prior (where the prior is symmetric and H_{\mathrm{unif}}(A^*) = \log K). Then the LLM prior yields a strictly tighter regret upper bound if and only if

H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) < \log K.

This condition is a priori verifiable: it depends only on the prior (LLM scores and the covariance structure) and the context distribution \mathcal{D}. One can estimate H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) by Monte Carlo simulation from the prior before deployment. If the LLM prior concentrates on a subset of arms, the entropy drops, and the regret constant shrinks. Conversely, an overconfident but wrong prior may have high entropy (or even exceed \log K), in which case the bound suggests no improvement—but the worst‑case guarantee of Theorem 3 still ensures safety.

---

4.3 Worst‑Case Frequentist Regret

We now drop the Bayesian assumption and allow an arbitrary, possibly adversarial, sequence of instances and costs. The forced exploration with rate \varepsilon and the conformal truncation yield a robust frequentist guarantee.

Theorem 3 (Frequentist Regret of CTS with Forced Exploration).
Set \varepsilon = \sqrt{\frac{K \log T}{T}}. Under Assumptions 1–4, the expected regret of CTS (Algorithm 1) satisfies

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\Big( \sqrt{K T \log T} + \alpha T \Big),

where \widetilde{\mathcal{O}} hides logarithmic factors. When \alpha is chosen as c/\sqrt{T}, the regret is \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{KT}).

Proof sketch. The proof adapts the standard analysis of linear Thompson sampling with forced exploration (Agrawal & Goyal, 2013). The conformal truncation delays learning only for arms outside \mathcal{C}_t, but Theorem 1 guarantees that the optimal arm is outside the set in at most \alpha T + \mathcal{O}(\sqrt{T}) rounds. On those rounds, the regret per step is at most 1. For rounds where a^* \in \mathcal{C}_t, we follow the same steps as in the unconstrained case, bounding the probability of selecting a suboptimal arm using the anti‑concentration properties of the Gaussian posterior. The forced exploration ensures each arm gets pulled enough to maintain accurate estimates. Summing the contributions yields the claimed bound. The full proof is in Appendix C. ∎

---

4.4 Summary of Theoretical Contributions

1. Conformal coverage: The algorithm maintains a user‑specified miscoverage rate adaptively, without exchangeability.
2. Bayesian regret and prior utility: The regret constant is directly proportional to the prior entropy of the optimal arm. The LLM prior is proven beneficial exactly when its entropy is below that of a uniform prior—a checkable condition.
3. Worst‑case robustness: Even if the LLM is completely wrong, the regret remains \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{KT}), matching the optimal rate for linear contextual bandits up to log factors.

