5. Related Work

Algorithm selection (Rice, 1976) has traditionally been approached with deterministic mappings or portfolios (Gomes & Selman, 2001). AutoML systems (Feurer et al., 2015) perform Bayesian optimization over algorithm configurations but do not provide decision‑time uncertainty quantification. Our work adds formal guarantees to the online selection problem.

Contextual bandits with Thompson sampling (Agrawal & Goyal, 2013; Russo & Van Roy, 2014) are the backbone of our design. The information‑theoretic analysis of Russo & Van Roy (2014) directly yields the connection between regret and prior entropy. Prior elicitation for bandits often requires manual feedback (Eckles & Kaptein, 2014); using an LLM to automatically produce a prior is novel.

Conformal prediction (Vovk et al., 2005; Angelopoulos & Bates, 2022) provides distribution‑free prediction sets. Adaptive conformal inference (Gibbs & Candès, 2021) extends the validity to non‑stationary streams, which we exploit. The integration of conformal sets directly into the action space of a bandit is a new idea; related but distinct is the use of conformal prediction for reward confidence intervals (Fannjiang et al., 2022).

LLMs for algorithm reasoning have been explored in tool selection (Yao et al., 2023; Schick et al., 2023) but lack calibration. Recent bandit‑driven LLM selection works (WWW 2024, arXiv:2508.09958) do not address partial observability or provide coverage guarantees. Our work fills this gap by formally coupling a conformally checked LLM prior with rigorous bandit guarantees.

