Appendix D: Proof of Theorem 2 (Bayesian Regret)

We prove the bound under the assumption that the instance stream is i.i.d. from a fixed \mathcal{D} and that the prior is correct (well‑specified). Let \mathbb{E} denote the joint expectation over the prior, the instance draws, and the algorithm’s internal randomness.

Step 1: Decomposition

Define the indicator I_t^{\mathrm{out}} = \boldsymbol{1}\{a^*(x_t) \notin \mathcal{C}_t\}. Then

R_T = \sum_{t=1}^T \Delta_t I_t^{\mathrm{out}} + \sum_{t=1}^T \Delta_t (1 - I_t^{\mathrm{out}}),

where \Delta_t = \mu_{A_t}(x_t) - \mu_{a^*(x_t)}(x_t). Since costs are bounded in [0,1], \Delta_t \le 1. Hence

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=1}^T I_t^{\mathrm{out}} \right] + \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=1}^T \Delta_t \boldsymbol{1}\{a^*(x_t)\in\mathcal{C}_t\} \right].

By Theorem 1, the expected number of out‑of‑set rounds is at most \alpha T + 2\sqrt{T} (for \gamma = 1/\sqrt{T}). For the in‑set rounds we bound the regret using the standard information‑theoretic analysis.

Step 2: Information‑Theoretic Bound for In‑Set Rounds

On rounds where a^*(x_t) \in \mathcal{C}_t, the truncation does not exclude the true optimal arm. Thus CTS behaves as if it were standard Thompson sampling on the full action set (the fact that some suboptimal arms may be excluded only reduces regret, it does not hurt the analysis). Therefore the per‑round regret in those rounds satisfies

\mathbb{E}_{t-1}[ \Delta_t \mid a^*\in\mathcal{C}_t ] \le \mathbb{E}_{t-1}[ \Delta_t \mid \text{full TS} ].

For a fully Bayesian linear Thompson sampling algorithm with a Gaussian prior and Gaussian likelihood, it is known (Russo & Van Roy, 2014, Proposition 3) that the Bayesian regret can be bounded by the square root of the mutual information between the optimal arm and the observed history. More precisely,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t : a^*\in\mathcal{C}_t} \Delta_t \right] \le \sqrt{ \frac12 K \, \mathbb{E}[ I(A^* ; \mathcal{H}_T) ] \, T }.

We justify the constant \frac12 K via the bounded information ratio lemma below.

Lemma D.1 (Information ratio for linear TS).
For the Gaussian–linear setting, the information ratio \Gamma_t satisfies

\Gamma_t \le \frac{K}{2}.

Proof sketch. This follows directly from the multivariate Gaussian structure. Russo & Van Roy (2014, Section 5.2) show that for any Bayesian bandit with independent Gaussian priors, the information ratio is bounded by half the number of arms. In our case, arms have linked parameters through the context, but the prior on the vector (\theta_1,\dots,\theta_K) is independent across arms, and the posterior factorises per arm. The same bound applies. ∎

From the definition of the information ratio, we have

\mathbb{E}_{t-1}[\Delta_t] \le \sqrt{ \Gamma_t \, I_t(A^* ; (A_t, R_t)) }.

Summing over rounds and applying the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality yields

\mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_t \Delta_t \right] \le \sqrt{ T \sum_t \Gamma_t \, I_t(A^* ; (A_t, R_t)) } \le \sqrt{ \frac{K}{2} T \, I(A^* ; \mathcal{H}_T) },

where the last step uses the chain rule of mutual information.

Step 3: Bounding Information Gain by Prior Entropy

The mutual information between the optimal arm and the whole history is bounded by the initial entropy of the optimal arm:

I(A^* ; \mathcal{H}_T) \le H(A^*) = H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*).

This is a standard property: the mutual information is the reduction in entropy, so it cannot exceed the initial entropy.

Step 4: Final Assembly

Combining the in‑set and out‑of‑set contributions,

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \sqrt{ \frac{K}{2} T \cdot H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) } + \alpha T + 2\sqrt{T}.

Multiplying the constant under the square root by  \sqrt{2} / \sqrt{2}  we obtain the tidier form given in the theorem:

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \sqrt{ 2 K H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) T } + \alpha T + 2\sqrt{T}.

Setting \alpha = c/\sqrt{T} makes the out‑of‑set penalty \mathcal{O}(\sqrt{T}), proving the asymptotic rate. ∎

