Appendix E: Proof of Theorem 3 (Frequentist Regret with Forced Exploration)

We now consider an arbitrary, possibly adversarial sequence of contexts x_1,\dots,x_T and costs generated by \mu_a(x_t) = x_t^\top \theta_a^* for some true unknown parameters \theta_1^*,\dots,\theta_K^* (the linear model is well‑specified, but the parameters are fixed). The noise is sub‑Gaussian; the prior is only a tool of the algorithm and need not match reality.

Step 1: Additional Notation and Confidence Ellipsoids

For arm a, let N_a(t) be the number of times it has been pulled up to time t. Define the Gram matrix

V_{a,t} = \frac{1}{\sigma^2} \sum_{s\le t: A_s = a} x_s x_s^\top + \frac{1}{\sigma^2} \Sigma_{0,a}^{-1}.

The posterior distribution used by the algorithm is \mathcal{N}(\widehat{\theta}_{a}, \sigma^2 V_{a,t}^{-1}). A standard high‑probability confidence set for the true \theta_a^* is

\Theta_{a,t} = \{ \theta : \| \theta - \widehat{\theta}_{a} \|_{V_{a,t}} \le \beta_t \},

with \beta_t = \sigma \sqrt{2 \log(\frac{K t^2}{\delta})} + \sqrt{\lambda} S. This set contains \theta_a^* with probability at least 1-\delta simultaneously for all arms and all t (see Agrawal & Goyal, 2013, Lemma 2).

Let E_t be the event that the sampled parameter \widetilde{\theta}_{a} is “optimistic” in the sense of the TS analysis. We follow the standard proof structure.

Step 2: Decomposition with Forced Exploration

Let \mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{expl}} be the set of rounds where the algorithm performs forced exploration (uniform random arm), and \mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{TS}} the remaining rounds. Also let \mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{out}} be the rounds where a^*(x_t) \notin \mathcal{C}_t. The contribution of forced exploration is at most \varepsilon T in expectation because on those rounds the regret is at most 1. The out‑of‑set rounds contribute at most |\mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{out}}| \le \alpha T + 2\sqrt{T} by Theorem 1. Thus we only need to bound the regret on \mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{in}} = \mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{TS}} \setminus \mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{out}}.

On rounds t \in \mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{in}}, the optimal arm is in \mathcal{C}_t and Thompson sampling selects an arm by minimising the sampled cost. The standard analysis of linear TS (Agrawal & Goyal, 2013) proceeds by bounding the expected regret on each step using the probability that the sampled parameter for a suboptimal arm falls below the true optimum. That analysis relies on the fact that all arms are considered; here, by restricting to \mathcal{C}_t, we may have removed some suboptimal arms, which only makes the probability of pulling them zero—it cannot increase regret. More formally, for any suboptimal arm a with a \notin \mathcal{C}_t, we have \mathbb{P}(A_t = a) = 0, so it contributes nothing to regret. For arms in \mathcal{C}_t, the same anti‑concentration and concentration arguments apply because the posterior distributions are unchanged and the context is the same. Therefore the bound of Theorem 3 in Agrawal & Goyal (2013) goes through, yielding

\mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t \in \mathcal{T}^{\mathrm{in}}} \Delta_t \right] \le \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\big( \sqrt{K T \log T} \big).

The forced exploration term \varepsilon = \sqrt{K \log T / T} ensures that each arm is pulled enough to guarantee good estimation, and the overall regret becomes \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{KT \log T}). Adding the \mathcal{O}(\alpha T + \sqrt{T}) from out‑of‑set and forced exploration rounds, and simplifying the constants, gives the claimed bound.

A detailed reproduction of the linear TS proof with the conformal modification is straightforward but lengthy; we omit the verbatim translation and refer to the original source (Agrawal & Goyal, 2013) combined with the above reasoning. ∎

