Appendix F: Prior Utility Condition and Entropy Estimation

Corollary 1 (Prior Utility Condition). The LLM prior improves the Bayesian regret bound compared to a uniform prior if and only if

H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) < H_{\mathrm{unif}}(A^*) = \log K.

Proof. For a uniform prior over the parameters (e.g., large isotropic Gaussians), the optimal arm’s entropy equals \log K because all arms are equally likely to be optimal a priori. The regret bound constant depends on \sqrt{H(A^*)}. Hence the improvement condition is immediate. ∎

Estimating H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) in practice. Before deployment, one can draw a large number of contexts x \sim \mathcal{D} (or a representative sample) and for each, draw parameters \theta from the LLM‑informed Gaussian prior. For each pair (x,\theta), the optimal arm is a = \arg\min_i x^\top \theta_i. The empirical distribution of optimal arms gives an estimate of the prior optimal‑arm distribution, and its entropy can be computed. This Monte Carlo estimate is consistent; because the prior is fully specified, this verification step is “a priori verifiable.”

