Appendix H: Model Misspecification Penalty (Optional)

When the linear model is misspecified—i.e., there is no \theta_a such that \mu_a(x) = x^\top \theta_a for all x—the analysis changes. If the best linear approximation in the RKHS (or in the Euclidean sense) has uniform error \xi, then the regret bound (both Bayesian and frequentist) acquires an unavoidable linear term 2\xi T. This is because the algorithm’s predictions are consistently biased by at most \xi, leading to a per‑round penalty. The complete bound becomes

\mathbb{E}[R_T] \le \sqrt{2 K H_{\mathrm{LLM}}(A^*) T} + \alpha T + 2\sqrt{T} + 2\xi T.

We state this without proof, referencing standard results (Srinivas et al., 2010; Russo & Van Roy, 2014). The framework thus remains transparent about the cost of feature insufficiency or model choice.

